<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Siewierz</h1><p class="st-meta">1443 CE – 1790 CE · capital Siewierz</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Silesian duchy owned for almost 350 years by the bishops of Krakow, who styled themselves dukes of Siewierz.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Siewierz, on the border of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-silesia">Silesia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-poland">Lesser Poland</a>, belonged to the Silesian duchies under Bohemian overlordship. In 1443 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zbigniew-olesnicki">Zbigniew Olesnicki</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Krakow</a> bought it from Duke Wenceslaus I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cieszyn">Cieszyn</a>, and from then on the bishops of Krakow ruled it as secular princes.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The duchy was a small territory with its own laws, courts and officials, ruled from a castle at Siewierz. Its position between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-silesia">Silesia</a> led to disputes about whether it belonged to the Polish Crown or the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia</a>. Its iron and lead workings brought income to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, who added the ducal title to their own.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1790 the Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> incorporated Siewierz into the Polish Crown, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>' secular rule. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-partition-of-poland">Third Partition</a> in 1795 it passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a>, and later to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-warsaw">Duchy of Warsaw</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/congress-poland">Congress Poland</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-siewierz.jpg" alt="Duchy of Siewierz" width="320" height="250"><figcaption>Image: Termer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SiewierskieIRP.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1443 CE – 1790 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Siewierz</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Zbigniew Olesnicki</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Polish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Poland</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Siewierz</dd></dl></aside></div>
